""}, {"type": "text", "content": ""}], "correct_feedback": "Correct!", "incorrect_feedback": [{"type": "text", "content": "Whoops! Something important is missing."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Maybe next time..."}], "allowed_attempts": 2}}, {"contents": [{"type": "skill", "content": "Reading"}, {"type": "skill", "content": "Writing"}, {"type": "skill", "content": "Speaking"}], "type": "learning_skills"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "Instructions: Complete the following steps. Fill in the Self-Assessment."}], "type": "post_instructions"}], "id": 12}], "stimuli": [{"template_override": 1, "id": 0, "_data": [], "contents": [{}], "type": "welcome"}, {"template_override": 1, "contents": [{"value": {"audiosrc": "enpe8n7le01ob03re2aud01", "images": [{"time": "100", "image": "enpe8n7le01ob03re2img01.jpg", "id": 0, "text": "<p><strong>Sarah:</strong>&nbsp;Professor, can I have a word with you?<br /> Professor: Of course. How may I help you?<br /> <strong>Sarah:</strong>&nbsp;It's just that I'm stuck on an essay I'm writing about technology negotiation. My hypothesis is basically that technology negotiation has become a huge problem for managers in companies.<br /> <strong>Professor:</strong>&nbsp;That's right. Nowadays executives are facing negotiations in an ambit that most people know very little about.</p>"}, {"time": "26500", "image": "enpe8n7le01ob03re2img02.jpg", "id": 1, "text": "<p><strong>Sarah:</strong>&nbsp;Are technology negotiations understood differently from other types of negotiation? I mean, shouldn't CEOs always hire experts to deal with this kind of negotiations?<br /> <strong>Professor:</strong>&nbsp;You are right. The complexity of the topic demands involved knowledge of technical aspects that most managers don't have.<br /> <strong>Sarah:</strong>&nbsp;But, isn't it necessary for all the people who are concerned with the negotiation to know the terminology at least? Shouldn't communication errors be avoided?<br /> <strong>Professor:</strong>&nbsp;I partly agree. Sometimes, the ego of those who are taught in technology make them think all the people should know what they are talking about and this often leads to misunderstandings.<br /> <strong>Sarah:</strong>&nbsp;I see your point. Now, I've found out that sometimes both parties think differently about how the technology will work. Aren't the estimates of the performance of a technology product promised at the first stage of the negotiations?<br /> <strong>Professor:</strong>&nbsp;In theory, yes. However, there is uncertainty when it comes to complex pieces of technology and it is not so easy to predict how they'll perform in a particular business.</p>"}, {"time": "103000", "image": "enpe8n7le01ob03re2img03.jpg", "id": 2, "text": "<p><strong>Sarah:</strong>&nbsp;What about staff training? Are the organizational changes included in the agreements?<br /> <strong>Professor:</strong>&nbsp;They should be. The implementation of new technology might derive conflict between both parties. Therefore, it is necessary for negotiators to try to foresee any possible hurdles in the implementation to avoid all these pitfalls.<br /> <strong>Sarah:</strong>&nbsp;I believe I've clarified my doubts, professor. You've been a great help. Thanks.<br /> <strong>Professor:</strong>&nbsp;Anytime!</p>"}], "coverimg": "enpe8n7le01ob03re2img01.jpg"}, "type": "slideshow"}], "type": "audio", "_data": [], "id": 1, "title": ""}], "id": 0, "_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "has_nav", "content": true}, {"type": "has_exercise_controls", "content": true}], "type": "lab_options"}], "type": "scholar"}]}